      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "The closest structural analogue, a 2-methyltryptamine amide linked to an aryl-heteroaryl group, showed an in vivo mouse brain-to-plasma ratio of 0.16 and was classified as BBB-permeable, directly supporting meaningful CNS access for the query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "Tropisetron, an indole-containing amide-like drug, reached a brain/plasma ratio of 2.5 in mice after subcutaneous dosing, demonstrating that indole amide scaffolds can achieve robust brain exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "N,N-dimethyltryptamine, a tryptamine derivative, entered the brain within seconds in rabbits and persisted in brain tissue, showing that the tryptamine core of the query is compatible with blood-brain barrier passage.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Tryptamine itself crossed the blood-brain barrier in rabbits, providing direct evidence that the unmodified tryptamine substructure in the query is CNS-penetrant.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a 2-methyltryptamine amide with an aryl-imidazole tail. Direct in vivo evidence from a close structural analogue (C04) shows a permeable brain-to-plasma ratio of 0.16, and additional tryptamine/indole amide analogues support CNS entry. No contradictory experimental evidence was identified, so the query is predicted to have meaningful CNS access.",
